"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][24]={"university_physics_en:M24-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M24-L1 – X-Ray Attenuation, Image Contrast and Absorbed Dose<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M24 – Bachelor: Medical Physics and Imaging<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M6: electromagnetic fields; M7:\noptics; M15: interactions of radiation with matter. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous module: M23-L7<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive a basic transmission\nmodel, calculate half-value thickness and distinguish absorbed dose from\nradiation-protection quantities.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>Why do bones often stand out from soft tissue in radiographs? An\nX-ray source illuminates the body and a detector records the transmitted\nintensity. Different materials attenuate photons by different amounts.\nThe detector signal is an intensity measurement, not a direct map of\nbiological effects. Photoelectric absorption and scattering can both\nremove photons from the detected beam; scattered photons may also\ndistort image contrast.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>For a narrow, monoenergetic and scatter-free beam, the probability of\ninteraction in a thin slab\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis proportional to the remaining intensity. Integrating\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dI=-\\mu I\\,dx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives exponential attenuation. Half-value thickness\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{1/2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the distance at which\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=I_0/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAbsorbed dose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nby contrast, is absorbed energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{\\mathrm{abs}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{Gy}=1\\,\\mathrm{J/kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>m<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I(x)=I_0e^{-\\mu x},\\qquad x_{1/2}=\\frac{\\ln 2}{\\mu},\\qquad D=\\frac{E_{\\mathrm{abs}}}{m}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For several homogeneous layers,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I/I_0=\\exp(-\\sum_j\\mu_j x_j)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\na spatially varying medium requires a line integral. Diagnostic X-ray\nbeams are generally polychromatic, so energy-dependent attenuation\nproduces beam hardening. Absorbed dose (Gy) and effective dose (Sv) are\ndistinct; one detector intensity cannot yield an individual’s biological\nrisk. Image quality also depends on photon statistics, scatter, detector\ncharacteristics and processing.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>Take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=0.20\\,\\mathrm{cm^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=5.0\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu x=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.368<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I/I_0=e^{-1}\\approx0.368<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe half-value thickness is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.20<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.47<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{1/2}=\\ln2/0.20\\approx3.47\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf a hypothetical\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.020<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.020\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis uniformly deposited in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.010<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=0.010\\,\\mathrm{Gy}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe drawing is purely an invented material model.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_321_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic X-ray source, slab and detector next to an exponentially decreasing normalised intensity curve.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic X-ray source, slab and detector\nnext to an exponentially decreasing normalised intensity\ncurve.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Original illustration of ideal monoenergetic, scatter-free\ntransmission; no patient measurements.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I/I_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu x=\\ln2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{1/2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=0.10\\,\\mathrm{cm^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is an absorbed dose of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{Gy}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot the same physical quantity as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{Sv}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I/I_0=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.693<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.10<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>6.93<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{1/2}=0.693/0.10\\approx6.93\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Gray measures deposited energy per mass; protective quantities in\nsieverts involve different definitions and weightings and cannot be read\ndirectly from Gy.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>The model neglects beam spectrum, multiple scattering and geometry.\nAll parameters are invented; they do not prescribe any clinical\nexposure. Medical use requires validated dosimetry, justification and\noptimisation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>X-ray attenuation depends on both the ray path and material\nproperties; absorbed dose is a separate energy-per-mass quantity.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Computed Tomography, Projections\nand Backprojection<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://inis.iaea.org/records/3edwt-0rf59\">IAEA INIS – Diagnostic radiology physics: handbook<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 12–23; metadata; handbook topic reference only, not formula verification; Subject relevance to lesson: M24-L1 – X-Ray Attenuation, Image Contrast and Absorbed Dose · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://nucleus.iaea.org/Pages/rpop.aspx\">IAEA NUCLEUS – Radiation Protection of Patients<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 32–38; topic reference only; Subject relevance to lesson: M24-L1 – X-Ray Attenuation, Image Contrast and Absorbed Dose · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M24-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M24-L2 – Computed Tomography, Projections and Backprojection<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M24 – Bachelor: Medical Physics and Imaging<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M7: ray optics; M8: Fourier\ntransforms; M17: numerical methods. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Turn X-ray transmission into a\nprojection and explain why unfiltered backprojection is blurred.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>A radiograph superimposes attenuation along each ray path. Computed\ntomography collects projections from many directions. Numerous\none-dimensional line integrals can then reconstruct a two-dimensional\nattenuation map. The challenge is not simply the number of measurements,\nbut their coverage of directions and spatial frequencies.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>For monoenergetic scatter-free radiation, the negative logarithm of\nnormalised intensity equals the line integral of the local attenuation\ncoefficient. In parallel-beam geometry with angle\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand detector coordinate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe Radon transform collects these integrals. The Fourier slice theorem\nidentifies the one-dimensional Fourier transform of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\theta(s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith a radial slice through the two-dimensional Fourier transform of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu(x,y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\theta(s)=-\\ln\\!\\left(\\frac{I_\\theta(s)}{I_0}\\right)=\\int_{\\ell(\\theta,s)}\\!\\mu(x,y)\\,dl.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Ordinary backprojection distributes each projection value along its\noriginal ray paths, over-emphasising low spatial frequencies and\nblurring edges. In ideal continuous two-dimensional parallel-beam\nreconstruction, filtered backprojection multiplies the Fourier transform\nof each projection by a ramp proportional to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\omega|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthen backprojects over\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le\\theta&lt;\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNormalisation depends on the Fourier convention. Actual fan-beam or\ncone-beam systems, stochastic detection, scatter and metal artifacts\nrequire modified models.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>For a line integral\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.80<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.80<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nideal transmission is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.449<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I/I_0=e^{-0.8}\\approx0.449<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf a ray crosses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_1=0.10\\,\\mathrm{cm^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_2=0.25\\,\\mathrm{cm^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe integral is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.30<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.80<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.30+0.50=0.80<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram shows invented ray geometry and conceptually compares plain\nwith filtered backprojection.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_062_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Rotating ideal X-ray source, several rays, sinogram strip and comparison of blurred with sharpened phantom boundaries.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Rotating ideal X-ray source, several\nrays, sinogram strip and comparison of blurred with sharpened phantom\nboundaries.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Schematic parallel-beam CT.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>No clinical projection data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What transmission corresponds to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=\\ln4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=0.12\\,\\mathrm{cm^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=5\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can a ramp filter increase noise in finite-count CT data?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I/I_0=e^{-\\ln4}=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.12<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.12\\cdot5=0.60<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The frequency weight\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\omega|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nboosts high spatial frequencies and their accompanying measurement\nnoise.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>The derivation assumes ideal parallel rays within a single slice.\nClinical CT accounts for beam hardening, scatter, angular sampling,\ndetector calibration and geometry-specific reconstruction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Projection data are negative logarithms of transmission.\nReconstruction requires a correct forward geometry and a controlled\ninversion.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Magnetic Resonance Imaging,\nLarmor Frequency and k-Space<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://lme.tf.fau.de/lecture-notes/lecture-notes-me/computed-tomography-reconstruction/\">FAU – FAU Pattern Recognition Lab – Computed Tomography: Reconstruction<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 55–87 and 102–103; Subject relevance to lesson: M24-L2 – Computed Tomography, Projections and Backprojection · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://inis.iaea.org/records/3edwt-0rf59\">IAEA INIS – Diagnostic radiology physics: handbook<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 12–23; metadata; handbook topic reference only, not formula verification; Subject relevance to lesson: M24-L2 – Computed Tomography, Projections and Backprojection · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M24-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M24-L3 – Magnetic Resonance Imaging, Larmor Frequency and k-Space<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M24 – Bachelor: Medical Physics and Imaging<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M6: magnetic fields; M11: nuclear\nspin; M8: Fourier transformation. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate Larmor frequency and\nspatial encoding for an ideal MRI sequence and distinguish k-space\nsamples from image pixels.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>Magnetic resonance imaging detects signals from nuclear spins in a\nstrong static magnetic field. Unlike X-rays and CT, it does not use\nionising X-ray radiation. A static field establishes magnetisation, and\nan appropriate radio-frequency pulse can tip it. While spins precess and\nrelax, their changing magnetisation induces a receive signal in a\ncoil.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>For a spin with gyromagnetic ratio\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe Larmor angular frequency is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_0=|\\gamma|B_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOrdinary frequency in hertz differs by a factor of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA time-dependent magnetic field gradient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑮<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncreates position-dependent phase\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑮<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>⋅<\/mo><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi(\\mathbf r,t)=\\gamma\\int_0^t\\mathbf G(\\tau)\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf r\\,d\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUnder a suitable sequence definition, this provides Fourier spatial\nencoding represented by the k-space vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf k(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe sign depends on the convention for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the complex receive signal.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>γ<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑮<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_0=|\\gamma|B_0,\\qquad f_0=\\frac{|\\gamma|B_0}{2\\pi},\\qquad \\mathbf k(t)=\\frac{\\gamma}{2\\pi}\\int_0^t\\mathbf G(\\tau)\\,d\\tau.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>An ideal 2D signal is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>d<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s(\\mathbf k)=\\int\\rho(\\mathbf r)e^{-i2\\pi\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf r}\\,d^2r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ρ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis an effective spin- and sequence-weighted signal density. Real signals\ndepend on T1 and T2 relaxation, susceptibility, motion, coil profiles\nand noise. k-space represents spatial frequency, not an anatomical\nimage; a suitable inverse Fourier transform produces the simplified\nspatial image.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>For protons,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>42.58<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\gamma|/(2\\pi)\\approx42.58\\,\\mathrm{MHz/T}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_0=3.0\\,\\mathrm T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>127.7<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_0\\approx127.7\\,\\mathrm{MHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA constant gradient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G=1.0\\,\\mathrm{mT/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmaintained for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.0\\,\\mathrm{ms}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>42.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k\\approx42.6\\,\\mathrm{m^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalong the relevant axis. The graphic connects precessing magnetisation,\nsampled k-space and an idealised image domain.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_185_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Precessing magnetisation in a static field and schematic k-space sampling grid leading to an image representation.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Precessing magnetisation in a static\nfield and schematic k-space sampling grid leading to an image\nrepresentation.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Conceptual MRI diagram without measured signals or patient\ndata.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find proton\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_0=1.5\\,\\mathrm T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G=2\\,\\mathrm{mT/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napplied for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t=1\\,\\mathrm{ms}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is angular frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot numerically equal to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin hertz?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>42.58<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>63.9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_0\\approx42.58\\cdot1.5=63.9\\,\\mathrm{MHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>42.58<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.002<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.001<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>85.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k\\approx(42.58\\cdot10^6)(0.002\\cdot0.001)\\approx85.2\\,\\mathrm{m^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">One revolution corresponds to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nradians:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_0=2\\pi f_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>The Fourier model assumes an ideal sequence, uniform reception and\ncorrected background field inhomogeneity. These calculations do not\nestablish patient safety limits or clinical image performance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>MRI encodes spatial information with controlled gradient-induced\nphase. The factor of 2π is essential to interpreting k-space.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Ultrasound, Pulse Echo and\nAcoustic Impedance<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://socr.umich.edu/BPAD1/BPAD1_notes/BPAD1_Chap04_MRI.html\">University of Michigan – Biomedical Physics with Applications to Disease, Chapter 4: MRI<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Eq. 6, 17–21; lines 166–170, 546–570; Subject relevance to lesson: M24-L3 – Magnetic Resonance Imaging, Larmor Frequency and k-Space · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://inis.iaea.org/records/3edwt-0rf59\">IAEA INIS – Diagnostic radiology physics: handbook<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 12–23; metadata; handbook topic reference only, not formula verification; Subject relevance to lesson: M24-L3 – Magnetic Resonance Imaging, Larmor Frequency and k-Space · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M24-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M24-L4 – Ultrasound, Pulse Echo and Acoustic Impedance<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M24 – Bachelor: Medical Physics and Imaging<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M4: sound waves and reflection; M16:\nmeasurement uncertainty. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive the pulse-echo depth\nformula, calculate intensity reflection from impedances and state model\nlimitations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>An ultrasound transducer sends short acoustic pulses into a medium.\nInterfaces between different acoustic properties generate echoes whose\ntravel time and intensity can be measured. A B-mode image assigns echoes\nto depths and beam directions; its brightness is therefore not a direct\ndensity map. Different echo delays also arise from different acoustic\npath lengths.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>If a pulse travels to an interface and back at an assumed constant\nsound speed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nits round trip over time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nobeys\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2d=ct<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAcoustic impedance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=\\rho c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the product of mass density and sound speed. For a plane interface at\nnormal incidence between lossless media, the fraction of reflected\nintensity is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the squared impedance expression. The amplitude reflection\ncoefficient is not squared and may carry a sign.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=\\frac{ct}{2},\\qquad Z=\\rho c,\\qquad R_I=\\left(\\frac{Z_2-Z_1}{Z_2+Z_1}\\right)^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The frequently assumed soft-tissue value of about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1540<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1540\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a useful teaching approximation, not a universal value for every\ntissue. Nonuniform sound speed produces depth errors. Shorter pulses\nimprove axial resolution, whereas higher frequency often increases\ntissue attenuation. Lateral resolution depends strongly on aperture,\nfocusing and diffraction. Real interfaces may also be oblique to the\nincident beam.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=100\\,\\mathrm{\\mu s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1540<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=1540\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe depth is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1540<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.077<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7.7<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=1540\\cdot100\\cdot10^{-6}/2=0.077\\,\\mathrm m=7.7\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_1=1.50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.65<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_2=1.65<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the same MRayl units,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.15<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3.15<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.00227<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_I=(0.15/3.15)^2\\approx0.00227<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.227<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.227\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram separates echo time from interface reflection.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_303_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Transducer, outgoing pulse, return echo from an interface and separated transmitted and received peaks versus time.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Transducer, outgoing pulse, return echo\nfrom an interface and separated transmitted and received peaks versus\ntime.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Original illustration of an ideal pulse-echo experiment.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>No clinical ultrasound data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the depth for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=80\\,\\mathrm{\\mu s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1540<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=1540\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_1=Z_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can a constant assumed sound speed cause errors?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1540<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.0616<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.16<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=1540\\cdot80\\cdot10^{-6}/2=0.0616\\,\\mathrm m=6.16\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_I=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nin the ideal model there is no reflection due to an impedance jump.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Tissues exhibit different sound speeds; mapping time to distance\nwith a single assumed speed may assign an incorrect depth.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>The intensity expression assumes plane waves, normal incidence and\nlossless interfaces. Multipath propagation, speckle, scattering,\nabsorption and speed gradients require richer models.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Pulse-echo depth contains a factor of one half for the return path.\nIdeal normal-incidence reflection depends on acoustic impedance\ncontrast.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Nuclear Medicine Imaging: PET\nand SPECT<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://dbauer.physics.ucla.edu/5b/23-ultrasound/\">UCLA – UCLA – Physics 5B, Ultrasound<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 63–80, 84–99; Subject relevance to lesson: M24-L4 – Ultrasound, Pulse Echo and Acoustic Impedance · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://inis.iaea.org/records/3edwt-0rf59\">IAEA INIS – Diagnostic radiology physics: handbook<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 12–23; metadata; handbook topic reference only, not formula verification; Subject relevance to lesson: M24-L4 – Ultrasound, Pulse Echo and Acoustic Impedance · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M24-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M24-L5 – Nuclear Medicine Imaging: PET and SPECT<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M24 – Bachelor: Medical Physics and Imaging<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M15: nuclear decay and radiation\ndetectors; M13: counting statistics. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish PET from SPECT\ndetection and evaluate ideal time-of-flight and counting-statistics\nexamples.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>PET and SPECT use the distribution of radioactive tracers to create\nimages. Both detect emitted radiation instead of transmitting an\nexternally generated X-ray beam through the body. Their images are\nestimates of activity distributions requiring corrections for decay,\nattenuation, scatter and detector response. Increased activity is not a\ndiagnosis without the appropriate clinical context.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>In positron emission tomography, a positron slows and annihilates\nwith an electron. When the pair has negligible net momentum, two nearly\nopposite photons each carry\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>511<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_ec^2\\approx511\\,\\mathrm{keV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCoincident detector events identify a line of response (LOR). In an\nideal time-of-flight model, an arrival-time difference\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncorresponds to a displacement\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x\\approx c\\Delta t/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalong the LOR. Single-photon emission computed tomography commonly\nrestricts individual gamma-photon directions with a mechanical\ncollimator.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>511<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mfrac><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><mi>N<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msqrt><mi>N<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\gamma=m_ec^2\\approx511\\,\\mathrm{keV},\\qquad \\Delta x\\approx\\frac{c\\,\\Delta t}{2},\\qquad \\frac{\\sigma_N}{N}\\approx\\frac{1}{\\sqrt N}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>PET photons are not exactly back-to-back because of residual particle\nmomentum. Positron range, scatter, random coincidences and detector\ntiming further limit localisation. SPECT photon energy depends on the\nradionuclide and is not universally 511 keV. For independent Poisson\ncounts of expectation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nstandard deviation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><msqrt><mi>N<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_N\\approx\\sqrt N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand relative uncertainty is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mi>N<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\sqrt N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is a statistical model, not a general benchmark of clinical image\nquality.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>At an assumed coincidence timing resolution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t=200\\,\\mathrm{ps}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe position uncertainty scale is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.030<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c\\Delta t/2\\approx0.030\\,\\mathrm m=3.0\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=10\\,000<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindependent expected events, the relative Poisson uncertainty is about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure compares a PET coincidence line with SPECT collimation and is\nnot patient imaging data.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_206_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Pair of opposite PET detectors and coincidence line on the left; SPECT mechanical collimator and single-photon detector on the right.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Pair of opposite PET detectors and\ncoincidence line on the left; SPECT mechanical collimator and\nsingle-photon detector on the right.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Original schematic of detector principles.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Not patient imaging data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t=100\\,\\mathrm{ps}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_N/N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>400<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=400<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must the energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>511<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">511\\,\\mathrm{keV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot be assigned to every SPECT examination?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.015<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x=3.0\\cdot10^8\\cdot10^{-10}/2=0.015\\,\\mathrm m=1.5\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>400<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\sqrt{400}=0.05=5\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">SPECT detects radionuclide-dependent gamma emissions; 511 keV is\ncharacteristic of annihilation photons in PET.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>The TOF model assumes ideal photon paths. Real reconstruction and\ndosimetry require multiple corrections and clinical validation. The\ncalculation is not a recommended protocol.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>PET uses detector coincidences to identify a response line; SPECT\noften uses collimated single photons. Counting statistics limit\nprecision in both.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Reconstruction, Resolution,\nNoise and Artifacts<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://socr.umich.edu/BPAD1/BPAD1_notes/BPAD1_Chap06_NuclearMedicine_PET_SPECT.html\">University of Michigan – Biomedical Physics with Applications to Disease, Chapter 6: Nuclear Medicine, PET and SPECT<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · sec. 6.4–6.5.2; lines 439, 451–465; Subject relevance to lesson: M24-L5 – Nuclear Medicine Imaging: PET and SPECT · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://inis.iaea.org/records/sy9wq-mvk34\">IAEA INIS – Nuclear Medicine Physics: handbook<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 12–15; metadata; handbook topic reference only, not formula verification; Subject relevance to lesson: M24-L5 – Nuclear Medicine Imaging: PET and SPECT · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://nucleus.iaea.org/Pages/rpop.aspx\">IAEA NUCLEUS – Radiation Protection of Patients<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 32–38; topic reference only; Subject relevance to lesson: M24-L5 – Nuclear Medicine Imaging: PET and SPECT · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M24-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M24-L6 – Reconstruction, Resolution, Noise and Artifacts<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M24 – Bachelor: Medical Physics and Imaging<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M8: convolution and Fourier\ntransforms; M16: error propagation; M17: inverse problems. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish image sharpness,\ncontrast, noise and artifacts quantitatively and methodologically.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>Medical imaging devices first generate raw signals. A physical\nforward model and a reconstruction algorithm then convert them into an\nimage. Images of the same underlying object may have very different\nnoise and apparent sharpness even though the measured structures are\nunchanged. Strong sharpening may create artifacts, so visual appeal\nalone does not establish quantitative image quality.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>In an approximately shift-invariant linear system, the true object\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(\\mathbf r)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis convolved with the point spread function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h(\\mathbf r)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand noise\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n(\\mathbf r)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis added. Convolution blurs edges and suppresses high spatial\nfrequencies. Two independent Gaussian blurs with standard deviations\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nadd in variance. For ideal Poisson counting with expectation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe ratio of mean to standard deviation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mi>N<\/mi><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mi>N<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N/\\sqrt N=\\sqrt N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><msqrt><mi>N<\/mi><\/msqrt><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(\\mathbf r)=(h*f)(\\mathbf r)+n(\\mathbf r),\\qquad \\mathrm{SNR}_{\\mathrm{counts}}\\approx\\sqrt N,\\qquad \\sigma_{\\mathrm{tot}}^2=\\sigma_1^2+\\sigma_2^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The counting formula does not universally describe reconstructed\npixels: filtering and iterative processing often create correlations\nbetween neighbouring pixels. Modulation transfer and sampling influence\nresolution; motion and inaccurate models cause additional artifacts.\nIncreasing photon counts may entail longer acquisition or greater\nexposure or activity depending on modality; maximising count statistics\nalone is not a clinical optimisation criterion.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>An invented detector collects on average\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>400<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=400<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nevents. Then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_N\\approx20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrelative uncertainty. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1600<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=1600<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nstandard deviation becomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>40<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">40<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand relative uncertainty falls to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.5\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nquadrupling the expected counts halves relative uncertainty. Two\nindependent Gaussian blurs with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_1=1\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_2=2\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncombine to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>5<\/mn><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.24<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_{\\mathrm{tot}}=\\sqrt5\\,\\mathrm{mm}\\approx2.24\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe curves are theoretical.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_169_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A schematic point source, narrow and broad model point spread functions and illustrative noise bars without patient data.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A schematic point source, narrow and\nbroad model point spread functions and illustrative noise bars without\npatient data.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Synthetic image-quality curves.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Not measured detector records.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is relative Poisson uncertainty for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_{\\mathrm{tot}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_1=3\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_2=4\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can correlations between reconstructed pixels invalidate a\ndirect\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mi>N<\/mi><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrule?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>100<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\sqrt{100}=10\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>9<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_{\\mathrm{tot}}=\\sqrt{9+16}\\,\\mathrm{mm}=5\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Algorithms combine measurements, introducing correlated errors; a\nreconstructed pixel is not an independent Poisson count.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>Shift invariance, linear response and Gaussian blur are\napproximations. Each imaging modality requires its own forward physics,\nquality metrics and safety considerations. The figure is entirely\nsynthetic.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Spatial resolution, noise and contrast are not interchangeable.\nImprovements must be checked against artifacts and acquisition\nconstraints.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Radiation Protection,\nJustification and Imaging Decisions<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://lme.tf.fau.de/lecture-notes/lecture-notes-me/computed-tomography-reconstruction/\">FAU – FAU Pattern Recognition Lab – Computed Tomography: Reconstruction<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 55–87 and 102–103; Subject relevance to lesson: M24-L6 – Reconstruction, Resolution, Noise and Artifacts · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://socr.umich.edu/BPAD1/BPAD1_notes/BPAD1_Chap06_NuclearMedicine_PET_SPECT.html\">University of Michigan – Biomedical Physics with Applications to Disease, Chapter 6: Nuclear Medicine, PET and SPECT<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · sec. 6.4–6.5.2; lines 439, 451–465; Subject relevance to lesson: M24-L6 – Reconstruction, Resolution, Noise and Artifacts · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://inis.iaea.org/records/3edwt-0rf59\">IAEA INIS – Diagnostic radiology physics: handbook<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 12–23; metadata; handbook topic reference only, not formula verification; Subject relevance to lesson: M24-L6 – Reconstruction, Resolution, Noise and Artifacts · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M24-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M24-L7 – Radiation Protection, Justification and Imaging Decisions<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M24 – Bachelor: Medical Physics and Imaging<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M15: interactions of radiation; M16:\nmeasurement uncertainty; M24-L1: dose terminology. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain clinical justification\nand optimisation in diagnostic imaging and distinguish physical dose\nfrom clinical judgement.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>Not every technically possible examination is medically justified.\nFor procedures involving ionising radiation, clinical benefit and\npotential detriment must be weighed before exposure. Acquisition\nconditions should then be optimised to obtain the needed diagnostic\ninformation at an appropriate exposure. This is not equivalent to\nminimising dose without regard to diagnostic image quality.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>Absorbed dose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nquantifies locally deposited energy per mass, expressed in gray.\nRadiation-protection quantities such as organ and effective dose involve\nadditional physical and population-based weighting and cannot be equated\nto an individual risk prediction. The IAEA international basic safety\nstandards distinguish justification of medical exposure from\noptimisation of protection. Technical parameters depend on the clinical\nquestion, modality, patient characteristics and required image\nquality.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>m<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=\\frac{E_{\\mathrm{abs}}}{m},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm{Gy}=1\\,\\mathrm{J\\,kg^{-1}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>X-rays and CT use externally generated ionising radiation; PET and\nSPECT use radioactive tracers, whose distribution and clearance affect\nexposure. MRI uses static and time-varying magnetic fields and\nradio-frequency energy; ultrasound uses mechanical waves. Absence of\nionising X-rays does not make MRI or ultrasound free of safety\nrequirements. Contraindications, equipment parameters and specialised\nsafety guidelines must still be considered. This lesson does not give\nclinical instructions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>For a purely mechanical example, hypothetical uniform deposition of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.020<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{\\mathrm{abs}}=0.020\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=2.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.010<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=0.010\\,\\mathrm{Gy}=10\\,\\mathrm{mGy}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis does not define an individual’s effective dose in Sv or justify an\nexamination. The diagram follows a conceptual path from a clinical\nquestion to method choice, documented justification, optimisation and\nquality review.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_171_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Neutral conceptual flow from clinical question to method selection, justification, optimisation and quality control.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Neutral conceptual flow from clinical\nquestion to method selection, justification, optimisation and quality\ncontrol.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Original conceptual quality assurance diagram; not a medical\ndecision algorithm.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.005<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{\\mathrm{abs}}=0.005\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndeposited in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the smallest technically attainable dose not always the\nrelevant optimisation target?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which methods do not themselves use ionising X-ray photons: CT, MRI,\nultrasound or PET?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.005<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=0.005\\,\\mathrm{Gy}=5\\,\\mathrm{mGy}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Diagnostic utility requires adequate information; too few measured\nevents may render an examination unusable.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">MRI and ultrasound; PET relies on radioactive emissions and CT on\nX-ray radiation.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>These principles do not replace national regulations, physician\njustification, radiation-protection expertise or equipment-specific\nsafety standards. All numbers are invented and are not examination\nsettings.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Justification addresses why an examination is needed; optimisation\naddresses how an already justified examination can supply useful\ninformation at an appropriate exposure.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://nucleus.iaea.org/sites/nss-oui/Published%20Collections/m_3761d926-c16f-4477-a63b-741a9db1c16c/m_3761d926-c16f-4477-a63b-741a9db1c16c__50_0.Html\">IAEA – IAEA International Basic Safety Standards<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · requirements 37–38, sec. 3.155 and 3.163–3.164; Subject relevance to lesson: M24-L7 – Radiation Protection, Justification and Imaging Decisions · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://nucleus.iaea.org/Pages/rpop.aspx\">IAEA NUCLEUS – Radiation Protection of Patients<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 32–38; topic reference only; Subject relevance to lesson: M24-L7 – Radiation Protection, Justification and Imaging Decisions · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://inis.iaea.org/records/3edwt-0rf59\">IAEA INIS – Diagnostic radiology physics: handbook<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 12–23; metadata; handbook topic reference only, not formula verification; Subject relevance to lesson: M24-L7 – Radiation Protection, Justification and Imaging Decisions · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
